# Lock-cleanup and git-lock debris (item diagnostic, 2026-10-14, run 194).
# Confirmed directly in the Cowork sandbox: unlink() on this FUSE-mounted
# working copy returns EPERM even on a file this session just created itself,
# while rename() and truncate() both succeed. That is a real, reproducible
# property of this mount, not a one-off or a Windows-side artefact. Every
# earlier run's "clear a stale lock by renaming/overwriting it" workaround was
# therefore correct, but each rename or probe file it left behind (to
# characterise the bug afresh) became a new piece of untracked debris,
# because nothing told git to stop caring about that shape of filename.
# These patterns are root-anchored (leading /) so they cannot catch a
# similarly-named file deliberately placed inside tools/, modules/ etc.
/.agent-lock.*
/.AGENT_LOG_*.md
/.tmp_log_combined.md
/AGENT_LOG.md.restored
/AGENT_LOG.md.testprobe
/.new_entry_*.md
/.new-entry-*.md
